**Reused Evidence**
None materially applicable from the earlier chapter dossiers to `5B`.

**New Evidence**
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Tough choices ahead for Russia's inflation hawk Nabiullina","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-03-17","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/tough-choices-ahead-for-russias-inflation-hawk-nabiullina-2786434","claim":"Reuters reported that Elvira Nabiullina resisted calls from powerful industrialists and the Economy Ministry for interest-rate cuts, and a cited economist said she must have had Putin's support to do this.","locator":"lines 415-426","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous reporting with named officials and quoted market participants; still mediated reporting, not a direct state record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022; early-war monetary-policy choice and ruler support signal within the period.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but meaningful: the article ties Nabiullina's technocratic stance to Putin's support, which is relevant to whether he empowered competent economic professionals.","contrary_evidence":["The central bank is institutionally independent, so this does not prove detailed Putin authorship of each policy move.","Some of the stability came from emergency macro measures and the shock itself, not broad prosperity policy."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.7"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin tips central bank chief Nabiullina for third term to ensure economic stability","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-03-18","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/russias-putin-proposes-to-reappoint-central-bank-chief-nabiullina-2787264","claim":"Putin proposed nominating Elvira Nabiullina for a third term in a bid to ensure macroeconomic stability, and Reuters noted she was a committed inflation fighter resisting pressure for rate cuts.","locator":"lines 406-413","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous report based on a Kremlin spokesman and clear attribution of the nomination proposal.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022; direct personnel decision during the sanctions shock.","ruler_attribution":"Direct on the nomination proposal; partially indirect on monetary-policy empowerment because the central bank sets rates independently, but Putin's retention choice mattered.","contrary_evidence":["The Bank of Russia still acted as an institution with some independence.","This says little about whether the broader economic agenda favored broad-based prosperity."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.3","5B.7"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Bank of Russia increases the key rate to 20% p.a.","publisher":"Bank of Russia","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://www.cbr.ru/eng/press/pr/?file=28022022_094500key_eng.htm","claim":"The Bank of Russia raised the key rate to 20% and said the move would support financial and price stability and protect citizens' savings after external conditions for the Russian economy changed drastically.","locator":"opening paragraph / press release body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary contemporaneous central-bank release with explicit policy action and rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022, immediately after the invasion and sanctions shock.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: the decision was taken by the central bank, not personally by Putin, but it occurred within the regime's crisis-management response and under his continued political authority.","contrary_evidence":["The emergency hike reflects crisis damage as much as stewardship.","Central-bank independence means this is not a pure Putin-authored action."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Bank of Russia cuts the key rate by 300 b.p. to 14.00% p.a.","publisher":"Bank of Russia","publication_date":"2022-04-29","url":"https://www.cbr.ru/eng/press/pr/?file=29042022_133000Key_eng.htm","claim":"The Bank of Russia cut the key rate to 14% because price and financial stability risks were no longer rising and weekly data showed a slowdown in price growth with a stronger ruble and cooler consumer activity.","locator":"opening paragraph / press release body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary contemporaneous central-bank release with explicit rationale and policy adjustment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022, showing policy response after the emergency phase.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: central-bank action rather than a direct Putin decree, but relevant to the economic team he kept in place.","contrary_evidence":["The easing followed a severe initial shock and import compression.","This does not by itself show broad-based prosperity."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Elvira Nabiullina’s speech at joint meeting of State Duma dedicated committees on Bank of Russia’s 2022 Annual Report","publisher":"Bank of Russia","publication_date":"2023-04-12","url":"https://cbr.ru/eng/press/event/?id=14701","claim":"Nabiullina said the urgent measures taken after the early-2022 sanctions shock helped prevent an inflationary spiral, avoid ruble depreciation, and let the financial system weather the storm with minimum losses.","locator":"lines 50-53","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official retrospective from the central bank head; strong for what the institution claims it achieved, but self-interested.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 target-period policy and outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant: it describes the regime's crisis-management apparatus under Putin, though the institution itself executed the measures.","contrary_evidence":["Self-reporting can overstate success.","It does not separate the effect of sanctions-driven import collapse from policy skill."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia to temporarily ban foreigners from selling assets","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-03-01","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/russia-to-impose-temporary-curbs-on-foreigners-seeking-to-exit-assets-2774884","claim":"Russia said it would temporarily stop foreign investors from selling Russian assets and would use its sovereign wealth fund to buy shares in Russian companies, framing the move as a response to sanctions.","locator":"lines 422-424","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a government decree and a prime-minister statement; contemporaneous and specific, though mediated.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022, immediately after the invasion.","ruler_attribution":"Partial-to-strong: the restriction was a state response under Putin's government, though the direct announcement came via the prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["Officials presented it as protecting jobs and making investors think carefully.","It may also be read as defensive crisis management rather than pure expropriation."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.9"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia moves towards nationalising assets of firms that leave - ruling party","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-03-09","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/russia-approves-first-step-towards-nationalising-assets-of-firms-that-leave--ruling-party-2781281","claim":"Reuters reported that United Russia said a government commission approved the first step toward nationalizing assets of foreign firms that leave, including a bill allowing firms more than 25% foreign-owned from unfriendly states to be put into external administration.","locator":"lines 425-428","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous report with clear legal-policy detail; the key claim is via ruling-party and commission statements.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022, during the sanctions-driven corporate exodus.","ruler_attribution":"Strong regime attribution: this was a Putin-era state response, even if the report cites the ruling party and commission rather than Putin personally.","contrary_evidence":["The government framed it as preventing bankruptcy and saving jobs.","It does not by itself prove Putin personally ordered every asset case."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin says Russia will enforce rouble payments for gas from Friday","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-04-01","url":"https://www.euronews.com/next/2022/04/01/ukraine-crisis-russia-gas-putin","claim":"Putin said he had signed a decree requiring foreign buyers to pay in roubles for Russian gas from April 1 or have contracts halted, explicitly tying the move to sovereignty and retaliation against sanctions.","locator":"opening paragraphs and quoted remarks","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting of Putin's own televised decree and remarks; direct attribution and clear policy content.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022, shortly after the invasion and sanctions.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: this is a Putin decree and public threat to contracts and supply terms.","contrary_evidence":["Putin claimed the move would strengthen sovereignty and preserve obligations.","The rouble strengthened partly because of this coercive demand, but the move also heightened energy-market instability."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.9"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"With troops in Ukraine, Russia's defence spending leaps 40%","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-05-19","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/with-troops-in-ukraine-russias-defence-spending-leaps-40-2828150","claim":"Russia's defence spending rose nearly 40% in the first four months of 2022, and the finance ministry said the budget was shifting from an expected surplus to at least a 1.6 trillion-ruble deficit because of sanctions and support payouts.","locator":"lines 420-423","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report based on preliminary finance-ministry data; strong for budget direction, not for ultimate long-run effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022, after the invasion and during wartime budget reallocation.","ruler_attribution":"Strong: the spending surge and deficit shift were consequences of Putin's war choice and his government's fiscal priorities.","contrary_evidence":["Some defense spending was a wartime necessity from the regime's own perspective.","A higher defense budget can be defended as security rather than prosperity policy, but it still crowds out civilian welfare."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Analysis: Surge in Russia's defence and security spending means cuts for schools and hospitals in 2023","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-11-22","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/surge-russias-defence-security-spending-means-cuts-schools-hospitals-2023-2022-11-22/","claim":"Reuters' budget analysis said Russia planned to spend nearly a third of the next year's budget on defence and domestic security while cutting funding for schools, hospitals, roads, national economy items, health, education, infrastructure, and R&D.","locator":"opening paragraphs and budget-analysis summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters analysis based on budget documents and independent expert commentary; strong on spending priorities, though forward-looking.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late 2022 target-period decision shaping the 2023 budget trajectory.","ruler_attribution":"Strong: the budget priorities reflect Putin's war-state priorities and his government's fiscal choices.","contrary_evidence":["The article notes social spending still rose somewhat in nominal terms.","Officials could argue wartime priorities and patriotic training were strategic, but they still reduced civilian productivity spending."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WEO Update July 2022 Press Briefing Transcript","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-07-27","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/07/27/tr072622-weo-uptate-july-22-press-briefing-transcript","claim":"IMF staff said the domestic economy in Russia had been doing fairly well, that measures taken at the onset of the war on the financial and monetary side were effective, and that strong oil and gas export revenues supported the outlook.","locator":"lines 355-360","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"High-quality multilateral staff assessment with direct, contemporaneous macro discussion; not ruler-authored but strong contextual evidence.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 midpoint assessment of the sanctions shock.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: the measures were implemented by Russian economic authorities, not a personal Putin statement, but they reflect the regime's crisis-management choices.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF still described the recession as severe.","The favorable near-term performance partly reflected export revenues and wartime distortions rather than broad prosperity."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia says budget deficit hit 2.3% of GDP in 2022","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2023-01-10","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/russia-says-budget-deficit-hit-23-of-gdp-in-2022-2977210","claim":"Russia recorded a budget deficit of 2.3% of GDP in 2022, reversing an earlier target surplus of 1% of GDP; the finance minister said the war burdened state finances and Putin had predicted a surplus only months earlier.","locator":"lines 418-423","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report based on finance-ministry remarks and the minister's own figures; strong for the fiscal result.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2022 target-year fiscal outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Strong: the deficit reflects the fiscal consequences of Putin's war and budget priorities, even if formal reporting came from the finance minister.","contrary_evidence":["Some deficit pressure came from external sanctions and price-cap policy.","Russia still had room to borrow and draw on reserves, which softened the immediate fiscal collapse."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian monthly inflation was 0.78% in December - Rosstat","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2023-01-13","url":"https://uk.investing.com/news/economy/russian-monthly-inflation-was-078-in-december--rosstat-2881415","claim":"Russia's annual inflation rate came in at 11.9% for 2022, according to Economy Minister Maxim Reshetnikov, after sharp price rises that followed sanctions imposed over the invasion of Ukraine.","locator":"lines 423-425","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report quoting the economy minister and Rosstat-reported price data; strong for the annual figure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2022 target-year inflation outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Strong on outcome, indirect on causation: the figure reflects the sanctions/invasion shock created by Putin's decision to invade.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation later eased from the spring surge.","Price controls and capital controls also helped contain the peak, so the annual number is not pure policy failure alone."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia's 2022 economic fall seen in lower incomes, slumping consumer demand","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2023-02-08","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/russas-2022-economic-fall-seen-in-lower-incomes-slumping-consumer-demand-2998520","claim":"Reuters reported that Russian consumer demand contracted at its fastest pace in seven years in 2022 and real disposable incomes fell 1%, with GDP down around 2.5% as sanctions and the war hit households.","locator":"lines 406-411","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters cites official data and links it to the sanctions/war context; strong for household outcome, though causal attribution is partly contextual.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2022 target-year household outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Strong on outcome and medium on causation: the drop is tied to Putin's war choice and sanctions response, though some effects were cushioned by state support and export revenues.","contrary_evidence":["Wages rose slightly in late 2022, so the household picture was mixed.","Some consumer-demand weakness could reflect wartime mobilization rather than only economic policy."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]} 
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian economy shrank by 1.2% in 2022, less than previously thought - stats service","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2023-12-29","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/russian-economy-shrank-by-12-in-2022-less-than-previously-thought--stats-service-3264757","claim":"Rosstat said Russia's economy contracted by 1.2% in 2022, and Reuters noted that Western sanctions followed Putin's decision to send troops into Ukraine.","locator":"opening paragraph / Reuters summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report of the official statistics-service revision; strong for the final 2022 GDP figure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2022 target-year GDP outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Strong on overall outcome attribution: the contraction is explicitly linked to Putin's invasion decision, though later revisions and wartime spending complicate exact magnitude.","contrary_evidence":["The final contraction was smaller than earlier estimates, so the immediate collapse was limited.","War spending later supported measured growth, but that does not change the 2022 loss in civilian prosperity."],"lenses":["5B.10"]} 

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across IMF, World Bank, Bank of Russia, Rosstat, Reuters/Euronews syndications, and a few legal/analysis pages.

Accepted because they added distinct ruler-linked economic evidence:
- Technocratic staffing and monetary credibility: Reuters on Nabiullina, Bank of Russia rate decisions, IMF press briefing, Bank of Russia annual-report speech.
- Property-rights and capital-control coercion: Reuters on foreign-asset restrictions, external administration, and rouble-for-gas decree.
- Wartime fiscal and welfare effects: Reuters on defence spending, budget reallocation, deficit, inflation, and household-income contraction.

Rejected as duplicates or lower-value overlap:
- Reuters/Euronews versions of the same rouble-payment and sanctions stories when they repeated the same decree without a materially new claim.
- The Reuters 7.5% rate-cut story and the Euronews Reuters item on relaxing non-resident curbs, which overlapped the emergency-rate/capital-control theme already captured by stronger CBR and IMF evidence.
- Later 2023 corporate-exit / asset-sale follow-ups when they did not add materially new 2022-period information beyond the March asset-control and nationalization measures.
- Broad World Bank and IMF global outlook pages when they did not add Russia-specific ruler attribution beyond context already captured by Russia-specific releases.
- Older baseline reports from before 2022 when they were useful only as background and not as target-period evidence.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence on whether Putin personally chose the technocratic response versus simply tolerating it. The Nabiullina coverage is strong, but some attribution remains indirect because the central bank operated through its own procedures.
- Better evidence on regional and household distribution inside Russia in 2022, especially who absorbed the losses and who benefited from subsidies, defense contracts, or energy windfalls.
- More granular evidence separating policy skill from external windfalls and sanctions compression, especially for the apparent short-run macro stabilization.
- Stronger direct evidence on productivity investment beyond the negative budget reallocation story, especially education, health, infrastructure, and innovation execution rather than appropriations alone.

More search would help on the first two gaps; the third and fourth are already directionally clear but still worth tightening if you want a more defensible later judge packet.
